The chapter's rule is to borrow while parsing if it reduces copying locally, but own the data once the message crosses a wider subsystem boundary, because a borrowed &str or Cow<'de, str> cannot outlive the buffer it points into. Start from this broken function that parses a local buffer and tries to return the borrowing struct:

    fn parse(bytes: &[u8]) -> BorrowedAudit<'_> {
        let text = String::from_utf8(bytes.to_vec()).unwrap(); // local buffer
        serde_json::from_str(&text).unwrap() // borrows `text`, dropped on return
    }

This does not compile: the returned struct borrows `text`, which is dropped when the function returns.

